
    <section xml:id="Math2000HW4">
      <title>Inversion Conversion</title>

      <exercise xml:id="hw4-inverses">
        <introduction>
          <p>Compute the inverses of the following matrices, or explain why they are not invertible:</p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <me>\begin{pmatrix}
                1 &amp; 3 &amp; 5 &amp; 7 \\
                11 &amp; 14 &amp; 21 &amp; 30 \\
                6 &amp; 12 &amp; 18 &amp; 24
              \end{pmatrix}</me>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <me>\begin{pmatrix}
                2 &amp; 2 &amp; 1 \\
                2 &amp; 1 &amp; 2 \\
                1 &amp; 2 &amp; 2
              \end{pmatrix}</me>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <me>\begin{pmatrix}
                2 &amp; 3 &amp; 2 &amp; 2 \\
                3 &amp; 2 &amp; 2 &amp; 2 \\
                3 &amp; 3 &amp; 2 &amp; 3 \\
                3 &amp; 3 &amp; 3 &amp; 2
              \end{pmatrix}</me>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="hw4-matrix-equation">
        <statement>
          <p>Consider the matrix equation:</p>
          <p>
            <me>A\begin{pmatrix}
              x_{1} \\ x_{2} \\ x_{3} \\ x_{4}
            \end{pmatrix}
            =\begin{pmatrix}
              1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \\ 1
            \end{pmatrix}.</me>
          </p>
          <p>Suppose that <m>A</m> is invertible and</p>
          <p>
            <me>A^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}
              0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\
              1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 \\
              0 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 \\
              0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0
            \end{pmatrix}.</me>
          </p>
          <p>What is the solution to the equation?</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="hw4-rank-nullity">
        <statement>
          <p>Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n\times n</m> matrix, and suppose it is invertible. What must its rank and nullity be? Explain your answer.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </section>

